<article class="env-remark" id="061T">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/061T">Remark <span data-tag="061T">10.69.7</span> <span class="named">(Other types of regular sequences)</span>.</a>  In the paper <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Kabele">Kabele</a>]</span> the author discusses two more regularity conditions for sequences $x_1, \ldots , x_ r$ of elements of a ring $R$. Namely, we say the sequence is <em>Koszul-regular</em> if $H_ i(K_{\bullet }(R, x_{\bullet })) = 0$ for $i \geq 1$ where $K_{\bullet }(R, x_{\bullet })$ is the Koszul complex. The sequence is called <em>$H_1$-regular</em> if $H_1(K_{\bullet }(R, x_{\bullet })) = 0$. One has the implications regular $\Rightarrow $ Koszul-regular $\Rightarrow $ $H_1$-regular $\Rightarrow $ quasi-regular. By examples the author shows that these implications cannot be reversed in general even if $R$ is a (non-Noetherian) local ring and the sequence generates the maximal ideal of $R$. We introduce these notions in more detail in More on Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/062D" data-tag="062D">15.31</a>. </p>
